<BaseLayout title="About" description="Reischa's biography as a professional bellydance performer, instructor, and choreographer in Nashville, TN.">
	<PageHero slot="hero" image={aboutHero} title="About Reischa" showTitle />

	<p class="lede">
		Reischa is a professional Bellydance Performer, Instructor, and
		Choreographer based in Nashville, Tennessee. She is known for her
		artistic and authentic representation of Raks Sharki. Her performances
		reflect a strong focus on Lebanese and Egyptian stylizations. She is
		one of the most sought-after dancers, especially within Nashville's
		Middle Eastern community.
	</p>
	<p>
		In addition to Raks Sharki, she has extensive training and experience
		in classical dance and acting. She began studying jazz and ballet
		during elementary school. Then, while in junior high, she began
		studying and performing ballet in a church group under Liz Dimmel.
		Through this experience, she learned the physical and creative
		benefits of dance. While in college, she continued studying jazz,
		ballet, flamenco, and eventually bellydance. She was then cast in the
		Tennessee Renaissance Fair as an actor, and had the opportunity to see
		a bellydance performance by Laylia White. This is when her love for
		the art form began.
	</p>
	<p>
		Her performances include many events, weddings, showers, parties, and
		restaurants. She has been a regular house dancer at venues such as
		Aladdin's Cafe, Memories Cafe, and Yanni's Greek Taverna. Her teaching
		experience includes 12 years at Vanderbilt University and Centennial
		Arts Center. Reischa holds a Bachelor of Art in Theatre Performance,
		with a minor in Voice. She has studied Middle Eastern Dance with many
		accomplished musicians such as George Kerdahi and dancers such as
		Laylia White, Aziza, and Suhaila Salimpour. She is thankful for those
		that have continuously given her opportunities to perform and grow in
		this art form. Shukran.
	</p>
	<a class="back-link" href="/about/resume/">Reischa's Full Resume &rarr;</a>
</BaseLayout>
